## Abstract (headline claim)
Randomized compiling (RC) alone or zero-noise extrapolation (ZNE) alone
give only limited error suppression for VQE energies under coherent
2-qubit-gate noise. Stacking RC + ZNE reduces the energy error by 1–2
orders of magnitude on H₂ and LiH (STO-3G), producing energies at or
below chemical accuracy across a range of noise strengths.

## Method (Sec. 2–3)
- Molecules: H₂, LiH in STO-3G.
- Ansatz: UCC-SD.
- Noise: coherent over-rotation on 2-qubit gates (Sec. 2.2).
- RC: standard Wallman-Emerson Pauli twirl, N_rand = 20.
- ZNE: linear extrapolation over scale factors [1, 2, 3] with gate folding.
- Energy: exact expectation from statevector / density matrix (paragraph
  above Fig. 4) OR finite-shot sampling.
- Optimizer: Powell (Fig. 4), also BOBYQA elsewhere.

## Results (Fig. 4, Fig. 5)
- Raw: tens of mHa at strong coherent noise.
- RC-only, ZNE-only: only slightly better than raw.
- RC+ZNE: ~1 mHa or below (inside chemical accuracy) across all tested
  ε values.

## Conclusion
The two mitigations are complementary: RC converts coherent errors into a
stochastic form that ZNE's linear extrapolation can then handle correctly.
The combined scheme is recommended for near-term VQE on devices where
coherent errors dominate.

